the AGENT CONTEXT: the server-side designation of the context whose
registered-and-active tools are served. distinct from the phase-2
resource scope (scope_add/...): resource scope filters the resources
LISTING; tool scope picks WHICH CONTEXT DEFINES THE TOOL SET. the
skills must teach the distinction (spec, skills impact).

advertised metadata (builtin table rows)
-----------------------------------------

name          tool_scope_show
description   "Show the current tool scope: which theory or repl
              context the server reads user-registered tools from,
              and the tools registered and active there. Tools are
              context entities in Isabelle: a tool is visible when
              the scope's context (transitively) imports its
              registering theory and it has not been deactivated
              (declare [[mcp_tool del: ...]] / closed bundle)."
inputSchema   {"type": "object"}
annotations   {readOnlyHint: true, idempotentHint: true,
               openWorldHint: false}

name          tool_scope_set
description   "Set the tool scope to a theory (by name, any known
              spelling) or to a repl (by id). Repl scope serves the
              tools of the repl's CURRENT state — use this after
              registering a tool via repl_step to call it without
              persisting the theory first."
inputSchema   {"type": "object", "properties":
                {"theory": {"type": "string"},
                 "repl": {"type": "string"}},
               ...exactly one of the two required (json schema:
               oneOf; described in prose in the property docs since
               some clients ignore oneOf)}
annotations   {readOnlyHint: false, idempotentHint: true,
               openWorldHint: false}
              (mutates connection state, not prover state)

name          tool_scope_include
description   "Open bundles in the current tool scope (like Isar's
              'context includes'): tools activated by those bundles
              become servable until the scope changes. Bundle names
              resolve in the scope's context."
inputSchema   {"type": "object", "properties":
                {"bundles": {"type": "array",
                             "items": {"type": "string"}}},
               "required": ["bundles"]}
annotations   {readOnlyHint: false, idempotentHint: true,
               openWorldHint: false}

name/argument mapping across the layers
----------------------------------------

  scala state       MCP_Server connection state: designation =
                    Theory(name) | Repl(id), plus included bundle
                    names (ordered). default: Theory(-T theory).
                    theory spellings normalize through the existing
                    image_theory resolver BEFORE storing (canonical
                    key crosses the bridge — the phase-2 rule).
  bridge            the designation argument of MCP.tools/MCP.run_tool
                    (plans/mcp_tool_registry) grows the repl form:
                    "theory:NAME" | "repl:ID"; a second argument
                    carries the include-bundle list (yxml strings).
  engine (ml)       dispatcher resolves: theory -> Thy_Info +
                    init_global; repl -> the repl's latest
                    Toplevel.state, Toplevel.context_of (VERIFIED
                    mid-proof by spike 8); then folds
                    Bundle.includes over the bundle names. bundle
                    resolution errors -> typed status error naming
                    the bundle.

exposure names: computed scala-side from the payload's full internal
names — extern-like shortening (base name when unambiguous within
the served set, else the qualified name) + the phase-2 sanitizer
(dots -> "__", regex ^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,64}$). pure function of the
row set, unit-testable against Fake_Backend; tools/call resolves
through the same function. builtins always win the bare name.

design decisions (from the spec, restated for implementation)
--------------------------------------------------------------

- load_theory does NOT auto-switch the designation (explicit only).
- included bundles are scope state, re-applied on every read, so a
  tool_scope_set replaces designation AND clears includes (state
  what you get: fresh context, no accumulated soup). include is
  additive until then.
- the repl designation reads the repl's CURRENT latest state at each
  tools/list — stepping the repl can change the tool set; that is
  the point (self-extension), and list_changed only fires on
  registration events, not on steps: document in the skill that
  tools/list after repl work is cheap and worth re-running.

